1. Decide what your CSV looks like

You need a clear format so your program can tell what’s a calibration point vs an unknown.

Simplest option: one CSV for everything with a “role” column.

Example column layout:
	•	sample_id – text label (“Std1”, “Std2”, “Sample A”…)
	•	role – "CAL" for calibration points, "UNK" for unknown samples
	•	concentration – numeric for CAL rows, blank or NA for UNK rows
	•	signal – numeric measured signal (RFU, OD, etc.)

So your CSV rows look like:

Your program logic will then be:
	•	“All rows where role == CAL and concentration + signal are filled → calibration data”
	•	“All rows where role == UNK and signal filled → unknowns”

You can absolutely tweak this (e.g., two separate CSVs), but this “role” column is very convenient once you start coding.

