3. How to structure this in your code (conceptually)

3.1. Split into layers/modules

To avoid spaghetti, mentally separate your app into three layers:
	1.	Data layer
	•	Responsible for: reading CSV, doing basic validation, splitting into calibration vs unknown.
	•	Inputs: file path from the GUI.
	•	Outputs:
	•	A collection of calibration points
	•	A collection of unknown points
	2.	Model/Math layer (the 4PL engine from earlier)
	•	Responsible for: fit 4PL, compute concentrations from signals.
	•	Inputs: arrays/lists of calibration concs and signals, unknown signals.
	•	Outputs:
	•	Fitted parameters A, B, C, D
	•	Estimated concentrations for unknowns
	3.	GUI layer
	•	Responsible for: window, buttons, text fields, labels, plot area.
	•	Talks to the data layer and model layer, but doesn’t do the heavy math itself.

You’ve already practiced this separation with your task-tracker project: backend logic in one place, Tkinter UI calling it.

