4. Data structures you’ll work with

Keep it simple, like you did with your task dictionaries.

After loading the CSV and splitting:
	•	Calibration data
A list of small dictionaries, for example:
	•	{"sample_id": "Std1", "concentration": 0.1, "signal": 150}
	•	{"sample_id": "Std2", "concentration": 0.5, "signal": 430}
	•	etc.
	•	Unknown data
A similar list:
	•	{"sample_id": "SampleA", "signal": 520}
	•	{"sample_id": "SampleB", "signal": 300}

From your perspective when coding:
	•	When you go to fit, you’ll build:
	•	A list/array of concentrations from the calibration list
	•	A list/array of signals from the calibration list
	•	When you go to calculate unknowns, you’ll loop over the unknown list and:
	•	take their signal
	•	push it through your inverse 4PL function
	•	add a new field like "estimated_concentration" to each unknown dictionary

These are all operations you’ve already done: lists, dicts, loops.

