5. GUI design – what’s on the window?

Given your experience with Tkinter, imagine it like this:

Top row – File loading
	•	Button “Load CSV…”
	•	When clicked: open file dialog, read CSV, parse.
	•	Label to show status:
	•	“No file loaded”
	•	Changes to “Loaded ‘calibration_data.csv’: 6 CAL rows, 3 UNK rows”

Middle – Data summary

You don’t need a full table widget at first. You could start with something simple like:
	•	A text box or label area that says:
	•	“Calibration points: 0.1, 0.5, 1, 5, 10 (5 points)”
	•	“Unknowns: SampleA, SampleB (2 samples)”
	•	Later, if you want, you can add a proper table (like Tkinter Treeview) to show the rows.

Right side / bottom – Controls and results

Controls:
	•	Button “Fit 4PL”
	•	When clicked:
	•	Grab calibration data currently in memory
	•	Call your model fitting
	•	Store A, B, C, D in your GUI object
	•	Button “Calculate unknowns”
	•	When clicked:
	•	Check that A, B, C, D exist (you’ve fitted)
	•	Loop through unknown samples, compute estimated concentrations
	•	Store those back into the unknown list

You could combine those into a single “Fit + calculate unknowns” button to keep it simpler.

Results:
	•	A small block showing:
	•	“A = …”
	•	“B = …”
	•	“C = …”
	•	“D = …”
	•	A text area or label listing:
	•	“SampleA: 2.3 ng/mL”
	•	“SampleB: 0.6 ng/mL”

Plot area

You have two choices:
	1.	Simpler to start: open the plot in a separate window
	•	When user clicks “Plot curve”:
	•	A graph pops up with the calibration curve + unknowns.
	2.	More integrated: embed the plot inside the Tkinter window
	•	This requires a bit more plumbing, but is nicer long-term:
	•	You get a dedicated frame area in the main window where the graph appears and updates.

I’d recommend starting with option 1 so you’re not wrestling with embedding while you’re still getting the math + CSV workflow working.

