6. How the events flow together

Let’s walk through the “button click chain” step-by-step so you can see what needs to happen in each callback:

When “Load CSV” is clicked
	1.	Get the file path from a file dialog.
	2.	Pass the file path to your CSV loader function in the data layer:
	•	It returns two things: a list of calibration dicts, a list of unknown dicts.
	3.	Store those lists as attributes in your GUI object / main controller.
	4.	Update status label:
	•	“Loaded calibration: 8 points; unknowns: 3 samples.”
	5.	Optionally, show a text preview of some rows.

When “Fit 4PL” is clicked
	1.	Check that you actually have calibration data loaded.
	2.	From the calibration list, build:
	•	A collection of concentration values
	•	A collection of signal values
	3.	Pass these to your fitting function in the model layer.
	4.	Get back A, B, C, D.
	5.	Store these parameters in your GUI object.
	6.	Update the label block to show these values.

When “Calculate unknowns” is clicked
	1.	Check that fitting has been done (A, B, C, D exist).
	2.	For each unknown in the unknown list:
	•	Get its signal
	•	Pass signal + A, B, C, D to your inverse 4PL function
	•	Store the resulting concentration back into that dictionary, e.g. under "estimated_concentration".
	3.	Update an output area (text box or label) with lines like:
	•	“SampleA: estimated concentration = 2.3 ng/mL”
	4.	Optionally, automatically call the plot function.

When “Plot curve” is clicked
	1.	Take calibration data (concs + signals).
	2.	Generate a smooth x-range and corresponding y values using your 4PL forward function.
	3.	Create a new figure:
	•	Scatter: calibration points (concentration vs signal).
	•	Line: smooth 4PL curve.
	4.	For each unknown:
	•	Plot a point at (estimated_concentration, measured signal).
	5.	Add axis labels and maybe a legend.

You already know how to think through loops and function calls, so this is just wiring those concepts to user actions.

